/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 26-30
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:26, name:"The Prophet on Trial", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 26:15",verse:"But know ye for certain, that if ye put me to death, ye shall surely bring innocent blood upon yourselves, and upon this city, and upon the inhabitants thereof: for of a truth the LORD hath sent me unto you to speak all these words in your ears."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah's trial is the first religious liberty trial recorded in Scripture, and the elders won his acquittal by quoting Micah's sermon from a century earlier (Jeremiah 26:17-18)?","Did you know Shiloh, the ruin Jeremiah named, was where the ark of God once stood before the Philistines captured it, so his hearers knew exactly what a fallen shrine looked like (Jeremiah 26:6)?","Did you know Urijah preached Jeremiah's very message but fled to Egypt and was executed, while Jeremiah stayed and was protected, showing that one prophet's fate is no rule for another (Jeremiah 26:20-24)?"], questions:[
{q:"When did the word of the LORD in this chapter come to Jeremiah?", options:["In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah","In the fourth year of the reign of Zedekiah","In the days of Hezekiah king of Judah","At the end of the reign of Josiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:1",verse:"In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah came this word from the LORD, saying,", insight:"God sent His warning at the start of a new reign; mercy speaks before judgment falls."},
{q:"Where did the LORD tell Jeremiah to stand and speak?", options:["In the court of the LORD's house","In the palace of the king","At the gate of the city","In the valley of Hinnom"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Stand in the court of the LORD’S house, and speak unto all the cities of Judah, which come to worship in the LORD’S house, all the words that I command thee to speak unto them; diminish not a word:", insight:"God sent the hard word to the place of worship, because those nearest the temple needed it most."},
{q:"To whom was Jeremiah told to speak?", options:["All the cities of Judah, which come to worship in the LORD's house","The king of Babylon and his captains","The priests of Shiloh","The ambassadors of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Stand in the court of the LORD’S house, and speak unto all the cities of Judah, which come to worship in the LORD’S house, all the words that I command thee to speak unto them; diminish not a word:", insight:"The message was for worshippers, a reminder that gathering at the temple cannot cover an unchanged heart."},
{q:"What did God hope would happen when Jeremiah preached?", options:["They would hearken, and turn every man from his evil way","They would build Him a greater temple","They would pay tribute to Babylon","They would flee from Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:3",verse:"If so be they will hearken, and turn every man from his evil way, that I may repent me of the evil, which I purpose to do unto them because of the evil of their doings.", insight:"Judgment was never God's first choice; He preached so that He might turn aside the evil He purposed."},
{q:"Whom had God sent to the people, again and again, without success?", options:["His servants the prophets, rising up early and sending them","The wise men of the east","The captains of the guard","The merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:5",verse:"To hearken to the words of my servants the prophets, whom I sent unto you, both rising up early, and sending them, but ye have not hearkened;", insight:"God is a God who rises early, sending messenger after messenger before He sends judgment."},
{q:"What did God say He would make the temple like?", options:["Like Shiloh","Like the tower of Babel","Like the garden of Eden","Like the tents of Kedar"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:6",verse:"Then will I make this house like Shiloh, and will make this city a curse to all the nations of the earth.", insight:"Shiloh was the ruined shrine where the ark once stood; God would let His own house fall if His people would not rise."},
{q:"Who heard Jeremiah speaking in the house of the LORD?", options:["The priests and the prophets and all the people","Only the king's servants","The elders of the land alone","No one, for he spoke in secret"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:7",verse:"So the priests and the prophets and all the people heard Jeremiah speaking these words in the house of the LORD.", insight:"The whole religious establishment heard the word, and the whole establishment rose against it."},
{q:"What did the crowd shout when Jeremiah finished speaking?", options:["Thou shalt surely die","Thou speakest well, prophet","Go in peace","Tell it to the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:8",verse:"Now it came to pass, when Jeremiah had made an end of speaking all that the LORD had commanded him to speak unto all the people, that the priests and the prophets and all the people took him, saying, Thou shalt surely die.", insight:"Truth telling has always been dangerous work; the crowd answered God's word with a death threat."},
{q:"What charge did the people bring against Jeremiah?", options:["That he prophesied the house would be like Shiloh and the city desolate","That he stole silver from the treasury","That he worshipped Baal in secret","That he conspired with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:9",verse:"Why hast thou prophesied in the name of the LORD, saying, This house shall be like Shiloh, and this city shall be desolate without an inhabitant? And all the people were gathered against Jeremiah in the house of the LORD.", insight:"They quoted his sermon back at him as a crime; to some hearers, prophecy itself is the offense."},
{q:"Where did the princes of Judah sit to hear Jeremiah's case?", options:["In the entry of the new gate of the LORD's house","In the king's throne room","At the well of the city","In the prison court"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:10",verse:"When the princes of Judah heard these things, then they came up from the king’s house unto the house of the LORD, and sat down in the entry of the new gate of the LORD’S house.", insight:"Even a hostile court sat in God's house; justice was done, for once, on holy ground."},
{q:"What verdict did the priests and prophets demand from the princes?", options:["This man is worthy to die","This man is a true prophet","This man deserves a reward","This man must be made a priest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:11",verse:"Then spake the priests and the prophets unto the princes and to all the people, saying, This man is worthy to die; for he hath prophesied against this city, as ye have heard with your ears.", insight:"The religious leaders demanded death for a sermon; never trust a title, test the heart."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah urge the people to do?", options:["Amend your ways and your doings, and obey the voice of the LORD","Stone the elders of the land","Flee at once to Babylon","Rebuild the broken walls"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:13",verse:"Therefore now amend your ways and your doings, and obey the voice of the LORD your God; and the LORD will repent him of the evil that he hath pronounced against you.", insight:"The way out was always open: amend, obey, and the LORD would turn aside the evil pronounced."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say about his own life at the trial?", options:["I am in your hand: do with me as seemeth good and meet unto you","I will call down fire from heaven","I appeal to Pharaoh for protection","I take back every word"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:14",verse:"As for me, behold, I am in your hand: do with me as seemeth good and meet unto you.", insight:"He rested his life in God's hands and their conscience, the quiet courage of a man with nothing to hide."},
{q:"What warning did Jeremiah give if they put him to death?", options:["They would bring innocent blood upon themselves, their city, and its people","The king would heap rewards on them","Babylon would surely spare them","Nothing at all would happen"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:15",verse:"But know ye for certain, that if ye put me to death, ye shall surely bring innocent blood upon yourselves, and upon this city, and upon the inhabitants thereof: for of a truth the LORD hath sent me unto you to speak all these words in your ears.", insight:"Killing the messenger does not kill the message; it only adds innocent blood to the charge."},
{q:"What did the princes and the people finally decide?", options:["This man is not worthy to die, for he hath spoken in the name of the LORD","This man must die at once","This man must be exiled to Egypt","This man must pay a heavy fine"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:16",verse:"Then said the princes and all the people unto the priests and to the prophets; This man is not worthy to die: for he hath spoken to us in the name of the LORD our God.", insight:"The princes and the people saw what the priests missed: a man speaking in God's name is not a criminal."},
{q:"Which earlier prophet did the elders quote to save Jeremiah?", options:["Micah the Morasthite, who prophesied in the days of Hezekiah","Elijah the Tishbite","Amos the herdsman of Tekoa","Jonah of Gath-hepher"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:18",verse:"Micah the Morasthite prophesied in the days of Hezekiah king of Judah, and spake to all the people of Judah, saying, Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Zion shall be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem shall become heaps, and the mountain of the house as the high places of a forest.", insight:"An old sermon, a century old, became a shield; God's word outlives the kings who hate it."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah answer Micah's hard prophecy?", options:["He feared the LORD, and besought Him, and the LORD turned aside the evil","He put Micah to death at once","He laughed the prophet to scorn","He fled to Egypt for help"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:19",verse:"Did Hezekiah king of Judah and all Judah put him at all to death? did he not fear the LORD, and besought the LORD, and the LORD repented him of the evil which he had pronounced against them? Thus might we procure great evil against our souls.", insight:"Hezekiah's pattern is the Bible's pattern: fear God, pray, and judgment turns aside."},
{q:"Who was the prophet that preached Jeremiah's same message against the city?", options:["Urijah the son of Shemaiah of Kirjath-jearim","Elhanan the son of Dodo","Shephatiah of the plain","Ira the Jairite"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:20",verse:"And there was also a man that prophesied in the name of the LORD, Urijah the son of Shemaiah of Kirjath-jearim, who prophesied against this city and against this land according to all the words of Jeremiah:", insight:"Jeremiah was not alone; God always keeps more witnesses than we know."},
{q:"Where did Urijah flee when Jehoiakim sought to kill him?", options:["Into Egypt","Into Babylon","Into the wilderness of Moab","Into the hill country of Ephraim"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:21",verse:"And when Jehoiakim the king, with all his mighty men, and all the princes, heard his words, the king sought to put him to death: but when Urijah heard it, he was afraid, and fled, and went into Egypt;", insight:"He ran to Egypt, the old house of bondage; fear often flees to the wrong refuge."},
{q:"Whose hand protected Jeremiah from being handed over to die?", options:["Ahikam the son of Shaphan","Gedaliah the son of Ahikam","Elnathan the son of Achbor","Gemariah the son of Hilkiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 26:24",verse:"Nevertheless the hand of Ahikam the son of Shaphan was with Jeremiah, that they should not give him into the hand of the people to put him to death.", insight:"God had a friend in the court; one faithful man in high places can turn the whole story."}
]},
{n:27, name:"The Yoke of Babylon", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 27:11",verse:"But the nations that bring their neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and serve him, those will I let remain still in their own land, saith the LORD; and they shall till it, and dwell therein."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah wore a wooden yoke on his neck as a living sermon, and ambassadors from five kingdoms carried the message home to their thrones (Jeremiah 27:2-3)?","Did you know God called Nebuchadnezzar 'my servant,' the same title He gave Moses and David, because even a pagan king serves God's purposes when He ordains it (Jeremiah 27:6)?","Did you know the bronze pillars, the great bronze sea, and the bases survived the first deportation, only to be carried to Babylon later, exactly as Jeremiah said (Jeremiah 27:19-22)?"], questions:[
{q:"When did this word come to Jeremiah?", options:["In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah","In the fourth year of Zedekiah","In the days of Hezekiah","After Jerusalem had fallen"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:1",verse:"In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah came this word unto Jeremiah from the LORD, saying,", insight:"God's yoke sermon came early in Jehoiakim's reign, years before the final fall; mercy warns long ahead of judgment."},
{q:"What visible sign was Jeremiah told to make and wear?", options:["Bonds and yokes, and put them upon his neck","An ephod and a breastplate","A scroll bound with cord","A crown of thorns"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD to me; Make thee bonds and yokes, and put them upon thy neck,", insight:"Jeremiah preached with wood on his neck; sometimes the sermon must be seen before it is heard."},
{q:"To which kings was Jeremiah told to send the yokes?", options:["Edom, Moab, Ammon, Tyre, and Zidon","Egypt, Assyria, Persia, and Elam","Judah, Israel, Samaria, and Galilee","Philistia, Gath, Ekron, and Ashdod"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:3",verse:"And send them to the king of Edom, and to the king of Moab, and to the king of the Ammonites, and to the king of Tyrus, and to the king of Zidon, by the hand of the messengers which come to Jerusalem unto Zedekiah king of Judah;", insight:"God's summons went to five neighboring thrones; no nation was exempt from the choice."},
{q:"In whose name were the messengers to speak to their masters?", options:["Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel","Thus saith the king of Judah","Thus saith the council of nations","Thus saith the priests of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:4",verse:"And command them to say unto their masters, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Thus shall ye say unto your masters;", insight:"The message bore heaven's seal, not Judah's politics; it was God speaking to kings."},
{q:"By what does God say He made the earth, man, and beast?", options:["By His great power and by His outstretched arm","By the counsel of the angels","By the hands of the kings","By the strength of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:5",verse:"I have made the earth, the man and the beast that are upon the ground, by my great power and by my outstretched arm, and have given it unto whom it seemed meet unto me.", insight:"The Creator's right to dispose of nations rests on the fact that He made them."},
{q:"Into whose hand had God given all these lands?", options:["Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon","Cyrus the king of Persia","Pharaoh the king of Egypt","Darius the Mede"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:6",verse:"And now have I given all these lands into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, my servant; and the beasts of the field have I given him also to serve him.", insight:"God gave the lands to Babylon; resisting God's appointed rod is resisting God."},
{q:"What surprising title did God give Nebuchadnezzar?", options:["My servant","My enemy","The destroyer","The anointed"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:6",verse:"And now have I given all these lands into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, my servant; and the beasts of the field have I given him also to serve him.", insight:"A pagan conqueror called God's servant; the Lord uses whom He will, and owes no one an explanation."},
{q:"How many generations of Babylon would the nations serve?", options:["Three: him, and his son, and his son's son","One only","Seven","Twelve"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:7",verse:"And all nations shall serve him, and his son, and his son’s son, until the very time of his land come: and then many nations and great kings shall serve themselves of him.", insight:"Babylon's day was numbered from the start; God sets both the rise and the fall of empires."},
{q:"What three punishments awaited the nation that refused Babylon's yoke?", options:["The sword, the famine, and the pestilence","Fire, hail, and darkness","Captivity, silence, and shame","Plague, locusts, and drought"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:8",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that the nation and kingdom which will not serve the same Nebuchadnezzar the king of Babylon, and that will not put their neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon, that nation will I punish, saith the LORD, with the sword, and with the famine, and with the pestilence, until I have consumed them by his hand.", insight:"God's judgments travel in threes through Jeremiah; refusal has a price, and it is named plainly."},
{q:"Which five voices did God tell the nations not to heed?", options:["Prophets, diviners, dreamers, enchanters, and sorcerers","Kings, priests, scribes, elders, and judges","Merchants, farmers, soldiers, singers, and builders","Fathers, mothers, sons, daughters, and servants"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:9",verse:"Therefore hearken not ye to your prophets, nor to your diviners, nor to your dreamers, nor to your enchanters, nor to your sorcerers, which speak unto you, saying, Ye shall not serve the king of Babylon:", insight:"When God speaks plainly, a crowd of flattering voices rises to drown Him out; learn their names."},
{q:"What would God do for the nation that brought its neck under Babylon's yoke?", options:["Let them remain still in their own land, to till it and dwell therein","Carry them captive at once","Burn their cities with fire","Give them Egypt's protection"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:11",verse:"But the nations that bring their neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and serve him, those will I let remain still in their own land, saith the LORD; and they shall till it, and dwell therein.", insight:"Submission to God's discipline was the way to stay home; the yoke was lighter than the sword."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah tell Zedekiah king of Judah to do?", options:["Bring your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and serve him and his people, and live","Gather your armies and fight","Flee to Egypt for horses","Shut the temple doors"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:12",verse:"I spake also to Zedekiah king of Judah according to all these words, saying, Bring your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and serve him and his people, and live.", insight:"The prophet's counsel to his own king was surrender; true patriotism sometimes wears a yoke."},
{q:"What did the false prophets keep telling the people?", options:["Ye shall not serve the king of Babylon","Babylon is already fallen","Egypt will save you","The vessels will return this year"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:14",verse:"Therefore hearken not unto the words of the prophets that speak unto you, saying, Ye shall not serve the king of Babylon: for they prophesy a lie unto you.", insight:"Their whole sermon was one sentence of comfort, and every word of it was a lie."},
{q:"What did God say would happen to the lying prophets themselves?", options:["They would perish with the people He drove out","They would be made priests","They would be spared in Egypt","They would see the restoration"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:15",verse:"For I have not sent them, saith the LORD, yet they prophesy a lie in my name; that I might drive you out, and that ye might perish, ye, and the prophets that prophesy unto you.", insight:"False prophets share the fate of their followers; leading others astray is a self-dug grave."},
{q:"What did the false prophets claim about the temple vessels?", options:["They shall now shortly be brought again from Babylon","They are hidden under the temple","They were melted down for tribute","They were never taken at all"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:16",verse:"Also I spake to the priests and to all this people, saying, Thus saith the LORD; Hearken not to the words of your prophets that prophesy unto you, saying, Behold, the vessels of the LORD’S house shall now shortly be brought again from Babylon: for they prophesy a lie unto you.", insight:"A 'shortly' with no warrant from God is still a lie, however sweet it sounds."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's simple counsel to the priests and people?", options:["Hearken not unto them; serve the king of Babylon, and live","Fight to the last man","Bribe the king of Egypt","Hide the vessels in caves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:17",verse:"Hearken not unto them; serve the king of Babylon, and live: wherefore should this city be laid waste?", insight:"'Serve and live': God's strange mercy, offering life through the very yoke they feared."},
{q:"What test did Jeremiah set for the prophets?", options:["Let them make intercession that the remaining vessels go not to Babylon","Let them predict the weather","Let them raise the dead","Let them speak in a foreign tongue"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:18",verse:"But if they be prophets, and if the word of the LORD be with them, let them now make intercession to the LORD of hosts, that the vessels which are left in the house of the LORD, and in the house of the king of Judah, and at Jerusalem, go not to Babylon.", insight:"True prophets pray; Jeremiah challenged the liars to do the one thing real prophets do."},
{q:"Which temple treasures did the LORD name as still remaining?", options:["The pillars, the sea, the bases, and the residue of the vessels","The ark, the mercy seat, and the cherubim","The golden altar and the candlesticks","The tables of the law"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:19",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts concerning the pillars, and concerning the sea, and concerning the bases, and concerning the residue of the vessels that remain in this city,", insight:"God knew every bronze pillar by name; nothing in His house escapes His accounting."},
{q:"Whom had Nebuchadnezzar carried captive when he left those vessels behind?", options:["Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim, and all the nobles of Judah and Jerusalem","Zedekiah and his sons","Jehoiakim and his army","The priests of Shiloh"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:20",verse:"Which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon took not, when he carried away captive Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim king of Judah from Jerusalem to Babylon, and all the nobles of Judah and Jerusalem;", insight:"The first deportation had already happened; the vessels left behind were a warning, not a comfort."},
{q:"What was God's final word about the remaining vessels?", options:["They shall be carried to Babylon, until the day I visit them; then will I bring them up and restore them","They shall be hidden forever","They shall be given to Egypt","They shall melt in the fire"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 27:22",verse:"They shall be carried to Babylon, and there shall they be until the day that I visit them, saith the LORD; then will I bring them up, and restore them to this place.", insight:"Even the vessels had an 'until'; God's 'then will I bring them up' outlasts every exile."}
]},
{n:28, name:"Hananiah's Lie", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 28:9",verse:"The prophet which prophesieth of peace, when the word of the prophet shall come to pass, then shall the prophet be known, that the LORD hath truly sent him."}, facts:["Did you know Hananiah's name means 'the LORD is gracious,' yet his 'gracious' message of quick deliverance was the lie that cost him his life (Jeremiah 28:15-17)?","Did you know Jeremiah first said 'Amen' to Hananiah's hopeful word, proving that a true prophet would rejoice if the easy message were true, before he tested it (Jeremiah 28:6-9)?","Did you know Hananiah smashed the wooden yoke in the fifth month and was dead by the seventh, living only about two months after his lie (Jeremiah 28:1, 17)?"], questions:[
{q:"In which year and month of Zedekiah's reign did Hananiah confront Jeremiah?", options:["The fourth year, the fifth month","The first year, the first month","The ninth year, the tenth month","The eleventh year, the fourth month"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:1",verse:"And it came to pass the same year, in the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah king of Judah, in the fourth year, and in the fifth month, that Hananiah the son of Azur the prophet, which was of Gibeon, spake unto me in the house of the LORD, in the presence of the priests and of all the people, saying,", insight:"The date is stamped on the lie; God's record keeps the calendar even when prophets will not keep the truth."},
{q:"Where was Hananiah the prophet from?", options:["Gibeon","Anathoth","Kirjath-jearim","Moresheth"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:1",verse:"And it came to pass the same year, in the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah king of Judah, in the fourth year, and in the fifth month, that Hananiah the son of Azur the prophet, which was of Gibeon, spake unto me in the house of the LORD, in the presence of the priests and of all the people, saying,", insight:"A hometown prophet with a hometown following; popularity is no proof of a calling."},
{q:"What did Hananiah claim God had done?", options:["Broken the yoke of the king of Babylon","Destroyed the city of Tyre","Opened the Red Sea again","Crowned Zedekiah forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:2",verse:"Thus speaketh the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel, saying, I have broken the yoke of the king of Babylon.", insight:"He preached the sermon everyone wanted; a crowd pleasing prophecy should make you check the text."},
{q:"Within what time did Hananiah promise the temple vessels would return?", options:["Within two full years","Within forty days","Within seventy years","Within one full year"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:3",verse:"Within two full years will I bring again into this place all the vessels of the LORD’S house, that Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon took away from this place, and carried them to Babylon:", insight:"He named a short, sweet deadline; false hope always hurries, true hope can wait."},
{q:"Whom did Hananiah say would come back from Babylon?", options:["Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim, with all the captives of Judah","Zedekiah and his princes","The priests of Shiloh","The army of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:4",verse:"And I will bring again to this place Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim king of Judah, with all the captives of Judah, that went into Babylon, saith the LORD: for I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon.", insight:"He promised the exiles home; comfort without repentance is the oldest lie in the pulpit."},
{q:"Before whom did Jeremiah answer Hananiah?", options:["The priests and all the people that stood in the house of the LORD","The king in his palace","The elders in secret","The captains at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:5",verse:"Then the prophet Jeremiah said unto the prophet Hananiah in the presence of the priests, and in the presence of all the people that stood in the house of the LORD,", insight:"The debate happened in God's house before God's people; truth does not fear an audience."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's first word in reply to Hananiah?", options:["Amen: the LORD do so","Thou liest","Be silent","Away with thee"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:6",verse:"Even the prophet Jeremiah said, Amen: the LORD do so: the LORD perform thy words which thou hast prophesied, to bring again the vessels of the LORD’S house, and all that is carried away captive, from Babylon into this place.", insight:"Jeremiah wished the easy word were true; a true prophet loves the people too much to enjoy their judgment."},
{q:"What did the true prophets of old prophesy against the nations?", options:["War, and evil, and pestilence","Peace, and plenty, and joy","Riches, and honor, and long life","Rain, and harvest, and rest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:8",verse:"The prophets that have been before me and before thee of old prophesied both against many countries, and against great kingdoms, of war, and of evil, and of pestilence.", insight:"The track record of true prophets is warning, not flattery; measure every new voice by the old ones."},
{q:"What is the test of a prophet who prophesies peace?", options:["When his word comes to pass, then shall he be known as truly sent","When the people applaud him","When the priests approve him","When his dreams are vivid"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:9",verse:"The prophet which prophesieth of peace, when the word of the prophet shall come to pass, then shall the prophet be known, that the LORD hath truly sent him.", insight:"Fulfillment is the final court; peace prophets are proved by events, not by eloquence."},
{q:"What did Hananiah do to the yoke on Jeremiah's neck?", options:["Took it off and brake it","Burned it with fire","Hung it on the temple door","Cast it into the river"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:10",verse:"Then Hananiah the prophet took the yoke from off the prophet Jeremiah’s neck, and brake it.", insight:"He smashed the sermon instead of answering it; when men cannot refute the word, they attack the sign."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah do when Hananiah broke his yoke?", options:["He went his way","He struck Hananiah down","He called down fire","He wept aloud"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:11",verse:"And Hananiah spake in the presence of all the people, saying, Thus saith the LORD; Even so will I break the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon from the neck of all nations within the space of two full years. And the prophet Jeremiah went his way.", insight:"He walked away; not every insult deserves an answer, and God's reply was already on its way."},
{q:"When did the word of the LORD come to Jeremiah against Hananiah?", options:["After Hananiah had broken the yoke from off Jeremiah's neck","Before Hananiah ever spoke","In the days of Jehoiakim","During the feast of tabernacles"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:12",verse:"Then the word of the LORD came unto Jeremiah the prophet, after that Hananiah the prophet had broken the yoke from off the neck of the prophet Jeremiah, saying,", insight:"God's answer followed the insult; heaven is never hurried and never late."},
{q:"What did God say about the broken wooden yokes?", options:["Thou hast broken the yokes of wood, but thou shalt make for them yokes of iron","They shall be mended with gold","They were never Mine to begin with","They shall rot in the field"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:13",verse:"Go and tell Hananiah, saying, Thus saith the LORD; Thou hast broken the yokes of wood; but thou shalt make for them yokes of iron.", insight:"Breaking God's sign only hardens the sentence; wood defied becomes iron imposed."},
{q:"What had God put upon the neck of all the nations?", options:["A yoke of iron","A chain of gold","A garland of flowers","A collar of silver"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:14",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; I have put a yoke of iron upon the neck of all these nations, that they may serve Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon; and they shall serve him: and I have given him the beasts of the field also.", insight:"Iron does not splinter like wood; the judgment Hananiah mocked was heavier than he knew."},
{q:"What else had God given to Nebuchadnezzar besides the nations?", options:["The beasts of the field also","The fish of the sea","The birds of the air","The stars of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:14",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; I have put a yoke of iron upon the neck of all these nations, that they may serve Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon; and they shall serve him: and I have given him the beasts of the field also.", insight:"God's grant to Babylon was total; when He gives, even the fields serve His servant."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say to Hananiah's face?", options:["The LORD hath not sent thee; but thou makest this people to trust in a lie","Thou art My beloved prophet","Thy words are sweeter than honey","The LORD bless thy ministry"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:15",verse:"Then said the prophet Jeremiah unto Hananiah the prophet, Hear now, Hananiah; The LORD hath not sent thee; but thou makest this people to trust in a lie.", insight:"The kindest thing Jeremiah could do was the hardest: name the lie and free the people trusting it."},
{q:"Why was Hananiah sentenced to die that year?", options:["Because he taught rebellion against the LORD","Because he stole from the temple","Because he fled to Egypt","Because he struck Jeremiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:16",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will cast thee from off the face of the earth: this year thou shalt die, because thou hast taught rebellion against the LORD.", insight:"Teaching rebellion against God is a capital crime in heaven's court; words have weight there."},
{q:"What did God say He would do with Hananiah?", options:["Cast him from off the face of the earth","Set him over the nations","Make him a true prophet","Send him to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:16",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will cast thee from off the face of the earth: this year thou shalt die, because thou hast taught rebellion against the LORD.", insight:"The man who promised quick return was himself quickly removed; God edits His story with a firm hand."},
{q:"In which month did Hananiah die?", options:["The seventh month","The fifth month","The first month","The twelfth month"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:17",verse:"So Hananiah the prophet died the same year in the seventh month.", insight:"He broke the yoke in the fifth month and died in the seventh; the lie lived about two months."},
{q:"How soon was God's word against Hananiah fulfilled?", options:["He died the same year","He lived forty more years","He died in his old age","He was never punished"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 28:17",verse:"So Hananiah the prophet died the same year in the seventh month.", insight:"'This year thou shalt die' needed no footnote; when God dates a sentence, the calendar obeys."}
]},
{n:29, name:"The Letter to the Exiles", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 29:11",verse:"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the LORD, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah told the exiles to pray for Babylon, the very city holding them captive, because 'in the peace thereof shall ye have peace' (Jeremiah 29:7)?","Did you know the seventy years were named while the exiles were still settling in, and Daniel was later found praying over this very letter as the years ran out (Jeremiah 29:10)?","Did you know Jeremiah 29:11, one of the most loved verses in the Bible, was written not to comfortable people but to exiles who would live and die in Babylon (Jeremiah 29:11)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did Jeremiah send from Jerusalem to the captives in Babylon?", options:["A letter","A yoke of wood","A scroll of curses","A golden vessel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:1",verse:"Now these are the words of the letter that Jeremiah the prophet sent from Jerusalem unto the residue of the elders which were carried away captives, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people whom Nebuchadnezzar had carried away captive from Jerusalem to Babylon;", insight:"God's word traveled by mail to exiles; distance from Jerusalem was no distance from God."},
{q:"To whom was Jeremiah's letter addressed?", options:["The elders, priests, prophets, and all the people carried captive to Babylon","Only to King Zedekiah","Only to the priests of the temple","To the king of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:1",verse:"Now these are the words of the letter that Jeremiah the prophet sent from Jerusalem unto the residue of the elders which were carried away captives, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people whom Nebuchadnezzar had carried away captive from Jerusalem to Babylon;", insight:"The letter went to the whole captive community; God's counsel is for all His people, not just the leaders."},
{q:"After whose departure from Jerusalem was the letter written?", options:["Jeconiah the king, the queen, the eunuchs, and the princes","Josiah the king","Zedekiah the king","Gedaliah the governor"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:2",verse:"(After that Jeconiah the king, and the queen, and the eunuchs, the princes of Judah and Jerusalem, and the carpenters, and the smiths, were departed from Jerusalem;)", insight:"The first deportation had already emptied the palace; the letter was for those living with loss."},
{q:"By whose hand was the letter carried to Babylon?", options:["Elasah the son of Shaphan, and Gemariah the son of Hilkiah","Elnathan the son of Achbor","Ahikam the son of Shaphan","Baruch the son of Neriah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:3",verse:"By the hand of Elasah the son of Shaphan, and Gemariah the son of Hilkiah, (whom Zedekiah king of Judah sent unto Babylon to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon) saying,", insight:"God names the mail carriers; no servant's errand is too small for Scripture to remember."},
{q:"What were the exiles told to do in Babylon?", options:["Build houses, dwell in them, plant gardens, and eat the fruit","Build an altar and offer sacrifices","Refuse all work and wait","Return secretly to Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:5",verse:"Build ye houses, and dwell in them; and plant gardens, and eat the fruit of them;", insight:"Settle in, plant, eat; faithfulness in exile looks like ordinary, rooted living."},
{q:"What did God command about family life in captivity?", options:["Marry, have children, and increase there, not diminished","Remain single until the return","Send the children back to Judah","Adopt the customs of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:6",verse:"Take ye wives, and beget sons and daughters; and take wives for your sons, and give your daughters to husbands, that they may bear sons and daughters; that ye may be increased there, and not diminished.", insight:"Grow the family even in a foreign land; God's people multiply in every soil."},
{q:"What were the exiles told to seek for the city of their captivity?", options:["The peace of the city, and to pray unto the LORD for it","Its destruction by fire","Its tribute of gold","Its alliance with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:7",verse:"And seek the peace of the city whither I have caused you to be carried away captives, and pray unto the LORD for it: for in the peace thereof shall ye have peace.", insight:"Pray for the city that enslaved you; your welfare is tied to your neighbor's, even in Babylon."},
{q:"Whom were the exiles warned not to believe?", options:["Their prophets and diviners who would deceive them","The elders of Jerusalem","The priests of the temple","The messengers of the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:8",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Let not your prophets and your diviners, that be in the midst of you, deceive you, neither hearken to your dreams which ye cause to be dreamed.", insight:"Babylon had its own flattering prophets; exile does not end the need for discernment."},
{q:"How long would the captivity in Babylon last?", options:["Seventy years","Two full years","Forty years","Seven years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:10",verse:"For thus saith the LORD, That after seventy years be accomplished at Babylon I will visit you, and perform my good word toward you, in causing you to return to this place.", insight:"God named the number while they were still unpacking; hope with a date steadies the heart."},
{q:"What did God say His thoughts toward the exiles were?", options:["Thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give them an expected end","Thoughts of anger and rejection","Thoughts of forgetting them","Thoughts of leaving them in Babylon forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:11",verse:"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the LORD, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end.", insight:"The most loved promise in Jeremiah was mailed to exiles; God's best thoughts find you in your worst place."},
{q:"What did God promise when the exiles called upon Him?", options:["He would hearken unto them","He would hide His face","He would answer only the priests","He would wait another seventy years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:12",verse:"Then shall ye call upon me, and ye shall go and pray unto me, and I will hearken unto you.", insight:"Prayer opens heaven even from Babylon; 'I will hearken' is God's standing offer."},
{q:"How must they seek God to find Him?", options:["Search for Him with all their heart","Bring a costly sacrifice","Journey to Jerusalem","Fast for forty days"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:13",verse:"And ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart.", insight:"Wholehearted seeking is the whole condition; God is found by those who want Him entirely."},
{q:"What did God promise to do with their captivity?", options:["Turn it away, and gather them from all the nations","Lengthen it to a hundred years","Scatter them further still","Leave them where they were"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:14",verse:"And I will be found of you, saith the LORD: and I will turn away your captivity, and I will gather you from all the nations, and from all the places whither I have driven you, saith the LORD; and I will bring you again into the place whence I caused you to be carried away captive.", insight:"Gathering is God's signature move; He who scattered will regather."},
{q:"To what did God compare those left behind in Jerusalem?", options:["Vile figs, that cannot be eaten, they are so evil","Good figs, fit for the king's table","Wheat ready for harvest","Grapes ripe for the press"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:17",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Behold, I will send upon them the sword, the famine, and the pestilence, and will make them like vile figs, that cannot be eaten, they are so evil.", insight:"Staying behind was no privilege; the vile figs rotted while the exiles were being preserved."},
{q:"Which two false prophets in Babylon did the LORD name?", options:["Ahab the son of Kolaiah, and Zedekiah the son of Maaseiah","Hananiah the son of Azur, and Shemaiah the Nehelamite","Eldad and Medad","Jannes and Jambres"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:21",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel, of Ahab the son of Kolaiah, and of Zedekiah the son of Maaseiah, which prophesy a lie unto you in my name; Behold, I will deliver them into the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon; and he shall slay them before your eyes;", insight:"God names names; anonymous comfort is easy, but heaven keeps a record."},
{q:"How did the king of Babylon execute Ahab and Zedekiah?", options:["He roasted them in the fire","He hanged them on a tree","He cast them into the lions' den","He exiled them to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:22",verse:"And of them shall be taken up a curse by all the captivity of Judah which are in Babylon, saying, The LORD make thee like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon roasted in the fire;", insight:"Their names became a curse on exiles' lips; a prophet's end preaches his last sermon."},
{q:"What villany had Ahab and Zedekiah committed in Israel?", options:["Adultery with their neighbours' wives, and lying words in God's name","Theft from the temple treasury","Murder of the priests","Worship of the sun"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:23",verse:"Because they have committed villany in Israel, and have committed adultery with their neighbours’ wives, and have spoken lying words in my name, which I have not commanded them; even I know, and am a witness, saith the LORD.", insight:"Their sins were double: defiled beds and defiled pulpits; God witnessed both."},
{q:"What did Shemaiah want the priest to do with Jeremiah?", options:["Put him in prison and in the stocks, as a madman","Crown him king","Send him to Babylon","Make him high priest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:26",verse:"The LORD hath made thee priest in the stead of Jehoiada the priest, that ye should be officers in the house of the LORD, for every man that is mad, and maketh himself a prophet, that thou shouldest put him in prison, and in the stocks.", insight:"He called God's prophet mad; the world has always had a cell ready for the truth-teller."},
{q:"How did Shemaiah describe the captivity in his letter?", options:["This captivity is long","This captivity is short","This captivity is over","This captivity is a dream"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:28",verse:"For therefore he sent unto us in Babylon, saying, This captivity is long: build ye houses, and dwell in them; and plant gardens, and eat the fruit of them.", insight:"Even the false prophet admitted the exile was long; truth leaks out of liars sometimes."},
{q:"What was Shemaiah the Nehelamite's punishment?", options:["He would have no man to dwell among the people, nor behold the good God would do","He would be made chief priest","He would return to Jerusalem in honor","He would be given great riches"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 29:32",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will punish Shemaiah the Nehelamite, and his seed: he shall not have a man to dwell among this people; neither shall he behold the good that I will do for my people, saith the LORD; because he hath taught rebellion against the LORD.", insight:"He taught rebellion and missed the restoration; opposing God's word costs you God's goodness."}
]},
{n:30, name:"The Time of Jacob's Trouble", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 30:7",verse:"Alas! for that day is great, so that none is like it: it is even the time of Jacob’s trouble; but he shall be saved out of it."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah 30 opens the 'Book of Consolation,' chapters 30 through 33, a scroll of pure comfort tucked between prophecies of judgment, which God told Jeremiah to write in a book (Jeremiah 30:2)?","Did you know 'the time of Jacob's trouble' is Scripture's name for a day of distress so great that 'none is like it,' yet the promise ends with 'he shall be saved out of it' (Jeremiah 30:7)?","Did you know Jeremiah 30:9 promises 'David their king, whom I will raise up,' a promise Christians see fulfilled in Jesus, the Son of David (Jeremiah 30:9)?"], questions:[
{q:"What was Jeremiah commanded to do with God's words?", options:["Write them all in a book","Speak them only once","Hide them in a jar","Teach them to the priests alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:2",verse:"Thus speaketh the LORD God of Israel, saying, Write thee all the words that I have spoken unto thee in a book.", insight:"Write it down; God's comfort was too precious to trust to memory alone."},
{q:"What promise opens this chapter?", options:["God would bring again the captivity of Israel and Judah, and they shall possess the land","The temple would never fall","Babylon would rule forever","The kings would never die"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:3",verse:"For, lo, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will bring again the captivity of my people Israel and Judah, saith the LORD: and I will cause them to return to the land that I gave to their fathers, and they shall possess it.", insight:"Before the trouble comes the promise; God frames the dark picture with coming light."},
{q:"What kind of voice had they heard?", options:["A voice of trembling, of fear, and not of peace","A voice of singing and joy","A voice of quiet rest","A voice of thunder from Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:5",verse:"For thus saith the LORD; We have heard a voice of trembling, of fear, and not of peace.", insight:"Honest prophecy names the fear first; only then can the comfort be believed."},
{q:"What strange sight did the LORD say He saw?", options:["Every man with his hands on his loins, as a woman in travail, faces turned pale","Men dancing with joy in the streets","Soldiers marching in triumph","Priests offering sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:6",verse:"Ask ye now, and see whether a man doth travail with child? wherefore do I see every man with his hands on his loins, as a woman in travail, and all faces are turned into paleness?", insight:"Strong men doubled over like women in labor; the day of trouble unmans the mighty."},
{q:"What name does Scripture give to that great and terrible day?", options:["The time of Jacob's trouble","The day of the LORD's wedding","The year of jubilee","The feast of harvest"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:7",verse:"Alas! for that day is great, so that none is like it: it is even the time of Jacob’s trouble; but he shall be saved out of it.", insight:"It bears Jacob's name because it is his family's story: trouble, then deliverance, then blessing."},
{q:"What hope closes the verse about Jacob's trouble?", options:["He shall be saved out of it","He shall perish in it","He shall flee to Egypt","He shall hide in caves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:7",verse:"Alas! for that day is great, so that none is like it: it is even the time of Jacob’s trouble; but he shall be saved out of it.", insight:"'But he shall be saved out of it': the trouble is real, and so is the rescue."},
{q:"What did God promise to break in that day?", options:["The yoke from off their neck, and burst their bonds","The temple pillars","The city walls","The covenant of old"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:8",verse:"For it shall come to pass in that day, saith the LORD of hosts, that I will break his yoke from off thy neck, and will burst thy bonds, and strangers shall no more serve themselves of him:", insight:"The yoke Jeremiah wore as a sermon would one day be shattered by the God who imposed it."},
{q:"Whom would the restored people serve?", options:["The LORD their God, and David their king, whom He would raise up","The king of Babylon forever","The priests of Shiloh","Themselves alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:9",verse:"But they shall serve the LORD their God, and David their king, whom I will raise up unto them.", insight:"'David their king' points beyond the exile to the greater Son of David, the true King."},
{q:"From where would God save Jacob and his seed?", options:["From afar, and from the land of their captivity","From the temple courts","From the mountains of Moab","From the ships of Tarshish"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:10",verse:"Therefore fear thou not, O my servant Jacob, saith the LORD; neither be dismayed, O Israel: for, lo, I will save thee from afar, and thy seed from the land of their captivity; and Jacob shall return, and shall be in rest, and be quiet, and none shall make him afraid.", insight:"'Fear thou not': distance cannot defeat a God who saves from afar."},
{q:"How would God correct His people?", options:["In measure, and not leave them altogether unpunished","Without any mercy at all","By destroying them fully","By ignoring their sins"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:11",verse:"For I am with thee, saith the LORD, to save thee: though I make a full end of all nations whither I have scattered thee, yet will I not make a full end of thee: but I will correct thee in measure, and will not leave thee altogether unpunished.", insight:"Correction in measure is love with limits; He disciplines His own but never destroys them."},
{q:"How did God describe their wound?", options:["Thy bruise is incurable, and thy wound is grievous","A scratch that will soon heal","A wound already forgotten","A bruise that never happened"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:12",verse:"For thus saith the LORD, Thy bruise is incurable, and thy wound is grievous.", insight:"Only God can name the wound truly; human remedies cannot touch what sin has broken."},
{q:"What did they lack for their healing?", options:["Any to plead their cause, and any healing medicines","Gold to pay the physicians","Friends to visit them","Time to recover"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:13",verse:"There is none to plead thy cause, that thou mayest be bound up: thou hast no healing medicines.", insight:"When no advocate pleads and no medicine heals, the case is ready for the Great Physician."},
{q:"Who had forgotten them in their affliction?", options:["All their lovers; they seek thee not","Their children","The prophets","The angels"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:14",verse:"All thy lovers have forgotten thee; they seek thee not; for I have wounded thee with the wound of an enemy, with the chastisement of a cruel one, for the multitude of thine iniquity; because thy sins were increased.", insight:"Fair-weather allies vanish in trouble; the wound of abandonment prepares the heart for the only Friend who stays."},
{q:"What would happen to all who devoured them?", options:["They shall be devoured, and their adversaries shall go into captivity","They shall be richly rewarded","They shall be forgiven at once","They shall rule forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:16",verse:"Therefore all they that devour thee shall be devoured; and all thine adversaries, every one of them, shall go into captivity; and they that spoil thee shall be a spoil, and all that prey upon thee will I give for a prey.", insight:"God's justice is symmetrical; the devourers will be devoured, the spoilers spoiled."},
{q:"What did God promise for their wounds?", options:["I will restore health unto thee, and heal thee of thy wounds","They shall remain forever","They shall be hidden from sight","They shall be avenged sevenfold"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:17",verse:"For I will restore health unto thee, and I will heal thee of thy wounds, saith the LORD; because they called thee an Outcast, saying, This is Zion, whom no man seeketh after.", insight:"The incurable bruise meets the unfailing Healer; 'I will restore health' is God's own prescription."},
{q:"What had the nations called Zion?", options:["An Outcast, whom no man seeketh after","The joy of the whole earth","The city of the great King","A praise among the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:17",verse:"For I will restore health unto thee, and I will heal thee of thy wounds, saith the LORD; because they called thee an Outcast, saying, This is Zion, whom no man seeketh after.", insight:"The world's label was 'Outcast'; God's answer was restoration, the divine reversal of every cruel name."},
{q:"What did God say about the ruined city?", options:["It shall be builded upon her own heap, and the palace remain after the manner thereof","It shall lie waste forever","It shall be given to strangers","It shall sink into the sea"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:18",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will bring again the captivity of Jacob’s tents, and have mercy on his dwellingplaces; and the city shall be builded upon her own heap, and the palace shall remain after the manner thereof.", insight:"Rebuilt on its own heap: God does not waste ruins; He builds the future on the very rubble."},
{q:"What would come out of the restored people?", options:["Thanksgiving and the voice of them that make merry","Weeping and wailing","Silence and shame","Anger and revenge"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:19",verse:"And out of them shall proceed thanksgiving and the voice of them that make merry: and I will multiply them, and they shall not be few; I will also glorify them, and they shall not be small.", insight:"The end of the story is a song; God turns the captives' sighs into thanksgiving."},
{q:"From where would their governor come?", options:["From the midst of them","From Babylon's court","From Egypt's palace","From a foreign land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:21",verse:"And their nobles shall be of themselves, and their governor shall proceed from the midst of them; and I will cause him to draw near, and he shall approach unto me: for who is this that engaged his heart to approach unto me? saith the LORD.", insight:"Their ruler would be one of their own; God raises leaders from within His people, not from their conquerors."},
{q:"What is the covenant promise that crowns the chapter?", options:["Ye shall be My people, and I will be your God","Ye shall be servants forever","Ye shall wander without rest","Ye shall be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 30:22",verse:"And ye shall be my people, and I will be your God.", insight:"The oldest promise in the Bible, renewed: belonging, mutual and everlasting."}
]}
]);
